A bare module name in the scope of an `open` of an input module may be a
submodule of the opened module, so it does not count toward an edge to
another library.
`ui` (an unwrapped library) defines `Tooltips` with a submodule
`ProjectBoost`. `Card` reads `ProjectBoost.saAudit` under a local
`Tooltips.( ... )`, under `let open` and in a pattern under
`Tooltips.( ... )`; `Badge` under a structure-level `open Tooltips`; `Chip`
after `include Tooltips`.
`app` depends on `ui` (it references `Card`) and happens to have its own
top-level module `ProjectBoost`. Only `app -> ui` is a real edge, so the
order is `ui`, then `app`, with no cycle:

  $ mkdir ui app

  $ cat > ui/tooltips.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css.config [("library-name", "ui")]]
  > module ProjectBoost = struct let saAudit = "audit" end
  > EOF

  $ cat > ui/card.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css.config [("library-name", "ui")]]
  > [@@@css ".ui-card{color:red;}"]
  > let a = Tooltips.(ProjectBoost.saAudit)
  > let b = let open Tooltips in ProjectBoost.saAudit
  > let c = match a with Tooltips.(ProjectBoost.(_)) -> b
  > EOF

  $ cat > ui/badge.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css.config [("library-name", "ui")]]
  > open Tooltips
  > let a = ProjectBoost.saAudit
  > EOF

  $ cat > ui/chip.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css.config [("library-name", "ui")]]
  > include Tooltips
  > let a = ProjectBoost.saAudit
  > EOF

  $ cat > app/projectBoost.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css.config [("library-name", "app")]]
  > [@@@css ".app-boost{color:blue;}"]
  > let _ = Card.a
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate --log info ui/tooltips.ml ui/card.ml ui/badge.ml ui/chip.ml app/projectBoost.ml
  styled-ppx: output file: stdout
  styled-ppx: library order: ui, app
  styled-ppx: order: ui: Tooltips, Badge, Card, Chip
  styled-ppx: order: app: ProjectBoost
  styled-ppx: environment: development
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .ui-card{color:red;}
  .app-boost{color:blue;}

An `open` of a module outside the input set (`Stdlib`, `Belt`, a
`Prelude` of an external library) cannot supply a module of another
library, so names in its scope still add edges. `app` opens `Stdlib`
and calls `Reset.init`, so `reset` comes first and the app's global rule
wins:

  $ mkdir reset app2

  $ cat > reset/reset.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css.config [("library-name", "reset")]]
  > [@@@css "a{color:inherit;}"]
  > let init () = ()
  > EOF

  $ cat > app2/main.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css.config [("library-name", "app2")]]
  > open Stdlib
  > [@@@css "a{color:blue;}"]
  > let () = Reset.init ()
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate --log info app2/main.ml reset/reset.ml
  styled-ppx: output file: stdout
  styled-ppx: library order: reset, app2
  styled-ppx: order: reset: Reset
  styled-ppx: order: app2: Main
  styled-ppx: environment: development
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  a{color:inherit;}
  a{color:blue;}
